\date{2025-03-04}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{Rebuilding My (Neo)Vim Config From Scratch}

\p{I used LazyVim for a long time, but I kept hitting a wall: understanding \em{how} LazyVim did something was consistently harder than if I had just written the thing myself. So I gave myself one day to rebuild from scratch, with the modest goal of first-class support for #{\TeX}, Python, Forester, and C/C++. That one-day sprint is long past, and the config has kept evolving since — this note describes where it landed, not the frantic first pass. The through-line is that almost everything now leans on Neovim's own primitives instead of a framework, which is exactly the transparency I was missing.}

\remark{The whole config lives in my [dotfiles](https://github.com/kkanarios32/dotfiles). It is a thin \code{init.lua} that loads four modules — \code{config.lazy}, \code{config.options}, \code{config.keymaps}, and \code{core.lsp} — with the actual plugin specs under \code{lua/plugins/} and per-server LSP tables under \code{lsp/}.}

\section{Sane defaults}{
\p{To my surprise, a lot of the behavior I had taken for granted turned out to be options LazyVim set internally. I was greeted on first launch by 8-space indents and a clipboard that would not survive a trip between terminal instances. So the first job was archaeology: I dug through LazyVim for every option I actually liked and copied them into \code{config/options.lua}. System clipboard, sensible folding via Treesitter (\code{foldexpr = v:lua.vim.treesitter.foldexpr()}), a global statusline, \code{conceallevel} for markup — the unglamorous foundation everything else sits on.}
}

\section{Plugin management}{
\p{The plugin manager is \code{lazy.nvim}, the de facto standard, and it genuinely just works. The bootstrap snippet from their docs clones it on first run, and after that every file under \code{lua/plugins/} that returns a spec table is imported automatically. No central manifest to keep in sync — a new file is a new plugin.}
}

\section{Native LSP, no framework}{
\p{This is where the config diverged most sharply from where it started. The first pass used \code{mason.nvim}, \code{mason-lspconfig}, and \code{nvim-lspconfig} — the standard stack. All three are now gone. Neovim 0.11 ships a [native LSP](https://neovim.io/doc/user/lsp/) configuration API: \code{vim.lsp.config} defines a server and \code{vim.lsp.enable} turns it on, and any table returned from \code{lsp/<name>.lua} on the runtime path is picked up as that server's config. So \code{core.lsp} is just a list —}
\codeblock{lua}{ vim.lsp.enable({ "forester", "texlab", "clangd", "basedpyright", "lua_ls", "ruff" })
}
\p{— and each server gets a plain Lua file in \code{lsp/}. No package manager, no bridge plugin translating between two config schemas. I install the servers themselves through my system package manager and let Neovim wire them up. It is dramatically less machinery for the same result, and when something breaks there is exactly one place to look.}
\p{The Python setup is the clearest upgrade. I dropped \code{pyright} for \code{basedpyright} and split responsibilities: \code{basedpyright} does type checking and inlay hints, while \code{ruff} runs as its own language server handling import organization and diagnostics. In the LazyVim days I fought constantly with import resolution, hand-writing a function to locate each project's virtualenv and pin \code{pythonPath} to its interpreter. \code{basedpyright} resolves environments well enough on its own that the hack is gone. \code{clangd}, \code{texlab}, and \code{lua_ls} round out the set, each configured in its own file — \code{texlab} in particular carries a fair amount of custom command wiring for building, forward search into Zathura, and environment manipulation.}
\p{A shared \code{LspAttach} autocommand in \code{core.lsp} binds the buffer-local LSP keymaps once, for every server: go-to-definition and friends route through \code{fzf-lua} pickers, and document highlighting, inlay-hint toggling, and native completion get enabled per-buffer based on what each client actually supports.}
}

\section{Forester finally has a [real language server](https://www.forester-notes.org/MZSF/index.xml)}{
\p{The original post described an ugly hack to get Forester completion. The \code{forester.nvim} plugin exposed a completion source, but only for \code{nvim-cmp}; \code{blink.cmp} (which I use — see below) needs a \code{blink.compat} shim, and that shim expected a plugin to \em{be} a source rather than to bury one inside a larger feature set. Getting reference completion working meant reading \code{blink.compat} internals and registering the source by hand.}
\p{None of that survives, because Forester now ships an actual language server. My \code{lsp/forester.lua} just launches \code{forester lsp} and hands it the forest root. The one wrinkle worth recording: \code{forester} locates the forest by its process working directory and \em{ignores} the LSP \code{rootUri}, and Neovim does not spawn the server in \code{root_dir} by default. So the config resolves the nearest \code{forest.toml} and starts the RPC process with an explicit \code{cwd} — otherwise the server exits with status 1 whenever Neovim's cwd has no \code{forest.toml} (e.g. when I open a tree from \code{$HOME}). Reference completion — indispensable for writing this very blog — now flows through the standard LSP path with no shim in sight.}
}

\section{Completion}{
\p{Completion is \code{blink.cmp}, the successor that finally displaced \code{nvim-cmp}. Its sources are \code{lsp}, \code{path}, \code{snippets}, and \code{omni}, with LuaSnip as the snippet engine. Because I lean on native LSP, the \code{on_attach} handler calls \code{vim.lsp.completion.enable} with autotrigger, so the server's completions feed the picker directly.}
\p{Layered on top is \code{supermaven-nvim} for AI ghost-text suggestions — accept-suggestion, accept-word, and clear bound to \code{<C-l>}, \code{<C-j>}, and \code{<C-\startverb]\stopverb>}. It fills the role Copilot-style tools play elsewhere without getting in the way of the structured LSP and snippet completions.}
}

\section{Snippets}{
\p{Going all the way back to the [Gilles Castel blog post](https://castel.dev/post/lecture-notes-1/), I have always wanted snippets that auto-expand. Under LazyVim I had settled for friendly-snippets on native Neovim snippets; rebuilding from scratch, I refused to compromise. The engine is \code{LuaSnip}, which \code{blink.cmp} supports natively, and I have to plug the [luasnip-latex-snippets repo](https://github.com/iurimateus/luasnip-latex-snippets.nvim) that ports the original UltiSnips math snippets over. My own snippets live in a small local plugin, \code{custom-luasnip-snippets.nvim}, vendored under \code{lua/local/}.}
\p{The trick I had wanted for years and can finally do: load LaTeX math snippets while inside math environments \em{in Forester}. The snippet plugin runs with \code{use_treesitter = true}, so it inspects the Forester Treesitter grammar, finds the node types for math environments, and only expands the math snippets when the cursor is actually inside one. Writing #{\TeX} inside a \code{.tree} file feels exactly like writing it in a \code{.tex} file.}
}

\section{Formatting and linting}{
\p{Formatting runs through \code{conform.nvim} on save. Python is handled by \code{ruff_format} followed by \code{pyink}, Lua by \code{stylua} — this replaced the old \code{black} + \code{isort} pairing, folding most of it into the same \code{ruff} that already runs as a language server. Formatting falls back to the LSP formatter when no dedicated tool is configured for a filetype.}
}

\section{Fuzzy finding and forest integration}{
\p{The fuzzy finder is \code{fzf-lua}, which replaced Telescope. File and grep pickers are scoped to a detected project root — a small \code{utils.root} module (lifted from LazyVim) walks LSP workspace folders, \code{.git}, and cwd to find it — with a dedicated \code{tex} override that restricts live-grep to #{\TeX} source files.}
\p{The piece I am most fond of is \code{forester-new}, a local plugin that wires the editor into the forest's own note pipeline. It shells out to \code{scripts/new} so every tree gets the standard frontmatter and routing, then commands like \code{:ForesterNew}, \code{:ForesterStub}, \code{:ForesterCitek}, and \code{:ForesterTransient} handle the follow-through: creating a tree and inserting a \code{\startverb\transclude\stopverb}, stubbing a concept and dropping a \code{\startverb[[id]]\stopverb} link at the cursor, or — in visual mode — lifting a selection into a new tree and replacing it in place with the transclude. Stub-and-link without ever leaving the sentence, which is exactly the friction the forest workflow is supposed to remove.}
}

\section{Anki}{
\p{I originally built a whole in-editor Anki workflow on top of [AnkiConnect](https://git.sr.ht/~foosoft/anki-connect#deck-actions) and a vendored \code{anki.nvim}. That has since moved out of the editor and into the forest itself: cards are just \code{\startverb\taxon{Card}\stopverb} trees, and \code{scripts/anki-sync} mirrors them into Anki — creating the note on first sync and updating in place thereafter. Writing a card is now the same as writing any other tree, snippets and all, and the sync is one command away. This is [an example of a card](2RO9).}
}
\comments{nvim}
